data: {"type":"handoff","handoff":{"text":"{\"v\":1,\"hash\":\"b9c4a57381ea9ddb699ee666755c58f30b5b91b61b304330fddf621f633a2b36\",\"at\":1794747286613,\"retried\":[],\"merged\":{\"byChunk\":[{\"chunkId\":1,\"points\":[{\"id\":\"1.1\",\"text\":\"Apollo 11 (July 16–24, 1969) was the American spaceflight that first landed humans on the Moon and the fifth crewed mission of NASA's Apollo program.\"},{\"id\":\"1.2\",\"text\":\"Commander Neil Armstrong, Command Module Pilot Michael Collins, and Lunar Module Pilot Buzz Aldrin were all on their second and final spaceflight.\"},{\"id\":\"1.3\",\"text\":\"The Apollo spacecraft had three parts: the command module (only part to return to Earth), the service module, and the Lunar Module.\"},{\"id\":\"1.4\",\"text\":\"The Lunar Module had a descent stage for landing and an ascent stage with a cabin for two astronauts and a small engine to return them to lunar orbit.\"},{\"id\":\"1.5\",\"text\":\"Armstrong and Aldrin landed the LM Eagle in the Sea of Tranquility on July 20 at 20:17 UTC while Collins remained in lunar orbit aboard Columbia.\"}],\"entities\":[\"Apollo 11\",\"NASA\",\"Apollo program\",\"Neil Armstrong\",\"Michael Collins\",\"Buzz Aldrin\",\"Saturn V\",\"Kennedy Space Center\",\"Lunar Module Eagle\",\"Command Module Columbia\",\"Sea of Tranquility\"]},{\"chunkId\":2,\"points\":[{\"id\":\"2.1\",\"text\":\"Armstrong walked on the Moon about six hours after landing, with Aldrin following nineteen minutes later.\"},{\"id\":\"2.2\",\"text\":\"The two spent roughly two and a half hours on the surface, planting a flag, speaking with President Nixon, and collecting 21.5 kg of lunar material.\"},{\"id\":\"2.3\",\"text\":\"The crew rejoined Collins in lunar orbit and returned to Earth, splashing down in the Pacific Ocean on July 24.\"},{\"id\":\"2.4\",\"text\":\"Apollo 11 culminated the Space Race, a Cold War competition between the United States and the Soviet Union.\"},{\"id\":\"2.5\",\"text\":\"President John F. Kennedy set the goal in May 1961 of landing a man on the Moon before the decade ended; the program overcame the fatal Apollo 1 fire in January 1967.\"}],\"entities\":[\"Armstrong\",\"Aldrin\",\"Collins\",\"Richard Nixon\",\"Apollo 11\",\"Space Race\",\"United States\",\"Soviet Union\",\"John F. Kennedy\",\"Apollo 1\"]},{\"chunkId\":3,\"points\":[{\"id\":\"3.1\",\"text\":\"The Soviet Union, despite early spaceflight leads, could not match the Saturn V rocket.\"},{\"id\":\"3.2\",\"text\":\"Soviet uncrewed probe Luna 15 crashed on the Moon on July 21, while Armstrong and Aldrin were still on the surface.\"},{\"id\":\"3.3\",\"text\":\"Armstrong's moonwalk was broadcast live to an estimated 600 million viewers, among the most-watched TV events in history.\"},{\"id\":\"3.4\",\"text\":\"Lunar samples led to identifying three previously unrecognized minerals, and surface instruments returned data for years.\"},{\"id\":\"3.5\",\"text\":\"The crew received ticker-tape parades, a world goodwill tour, and the Presidential Medal of Freedom; Columbia is at the National Air and Space Museum.\"}],\"entities\":[\"Soviet Union\",\"Luna 15\",\"Neil Armstrong\",\"Buzz Aldrin\",\"Saturn V\",\"Columbia\",\"National Air and Space Museum\",\"Eagle\",\"Presidential Medal of Freedom\",\"Cold War\"]},{\"chunkId\":4,\"points\":[{\"id\":\"4.1\",\"text\":\"On October 4, 1957, the Soviet Union launched Sputnik 1, the first artificial satellite, surprising the world and fueling fears about Soviet technological and military capabilities.\"},{\"id\":\"4.2\",\"text\":\"Sputnik's success suggested the USSR could potentially deliver nuclear weapons over intercontinental distances, challenging American claims of military, economic, and technological superiority.\"},{\"id\":\"4.3\",\"text\":\"Sputnik sparked the Sputnik crisis and ignited the Space Race, as both superpowers sought to demonstrate superiority in spaceflight.\"},{\"id\":\"4.4\",\"text\":\"President Dwight D. Eisenhower responded by creating NASA and initiating Project Mercury, which aimed to place a man into Earth orbit.\"},{\"id\":\"4.5\",\"text\":\"On April 12, 1961, cosmonaut Yuri Gagarin became the first person in space and first to orbit the Earth. On May 5, 1961, Alan Shepard became the first American in space on a suborbital flight.\"}],\"entities\":[\"Soviet Union\",\"Sputnik 1\",\"Sputnik crisis\",\"Space Race\",\"Dwight D. Eisenhower\",\"National Aeronautics and Space Administration (NASA)\",\"Project Mercury\",\"Yuri Gagarin\",\"Alan Shepard\"]},{\"chunkId\":5,\"points\":[{\"id\":\"5.1\",\"text\":\"Kennedy, Eisenhower's successor, chose a challenge beyond existing rockets so both countries would start from an equal position.\"},{\"id\":\"5.2\",\"text\":\"A crewed Moon mission was presented as the challenge that would serve this purpose.\"},{\"id\":\"5.3\",\"text\":\"On May 25, 1961, Kennedy told Congress the nation should commit to landing a man on the Moon and returning him safely before the 1960s end.\"},{\"id\":\"5.4\",\"text\":\"Kennedy said no single space project would be more impressive to mankind, or more important for long-range space exploration, and none so difficult or expensive.\"},{\"id\":\"5.5\",\"text\":\"Kennedy proposed accelerating lunar spacecraft development, developing alternate liquid and solid fuel boosters, and funding engine development and unmanned exploration.\"}],\"entities\":[\"Kennedy\",\"Eisenhower\",\"United States Congress\",\"Moon\",\"Earth\"]},{\"chunkId\":6,\"points\":[{\"id\":\"6.1\",\"text\":\"Kennedy says reaching the Moon will require an entire nation's effort, since all Americans must work to put one man there.\"},{\"id\":\"6.2\",\"text\":\"On September 12, 1962, Kennedy gave a speech to about 40,000 people at Rice University football stadium in Houston, Texas.\"},{\"id\":\"6.3\",\"text\":\"Kennedy argues outer space has no strife, prejudice, or national conflict yet, and its hazards are hostile to all mankind.\"},{\"id\":\"6.4\",\"text\":\"Kennedy says conquering space deserves the best of all mankind and its chance for peaceful cooperation may never come again.\"},{\"id\":\"6.5\",\"text\":\"Kennedy answers 'why the Moon?' by comparing it to climbing the highest mountain, flying the Atlantic, and Rice playing Texas, ending with 'We choose to go to the Moon.'\"}],\"entities\":[\"Kennedy\",\"Rice University\",\"Houston, Texas\",\"Moon\",\"Texas\"]},{\"chunkId\":7,\"points\":[{\"id\":\"7.1\",\"text\":\"A quoted statement says the Moon goal is chosen because it is hard, to organize the best energies and skills and as a challenge to win.\"},{\"id\":\"7.2\",\"text\":\"The proposed program faced widespread opposition; Norbert Wiener of MIT dubbed it a \\\"moondoggle.\\\"\"},{\"id\":\"7.3\",\"text\":\"The Moon landing effort already had the name Project Apollo.\"},{\"id\":\"7.4\",\"text\":\"Kennedy proposed a joint Moon landing to Khrushchev in June 1961, but Khrushchev declined; Kennedy re-proposed it at the UN on September 20, 1963.\"},{\"id\":\"7.5\",\"text\":\"The joint Moon mission idea was ultimately abandoned after Kennedy's death, and an early key decision chose lunar orbit rendezvous over direct ascent and Earth orbit rendezvous.\"}],\"entities\":[\"Norbert Wiener\",\"Massachusetts Institute of Technology\",\"Project Apollo\",\"Kennedy\",\"Nikita Khrushchev\",\"Soviet Union\",\"United Nations General Assembly\",\"lunar orbit rendezvous\",\"Moon\"]},{\"chunkId\":8,\"points\":[{\"id\":\"8.1\",\"text\":\"In July 1962, NASA head James Webb announced lunar orbit rendezvous would be used for the Apollo mission.\"},{\"id\":\"8.2\",\"text\":\"The Apollo spacecraft had three major parts: command module, service module, and lunar module.\"},{\"id\":\"8.3\",\"text\":\"The command module housed the three astronauts and was the only part returning to Earth.\"},{\"id\":\"8.4\",\"text\":\"The lunar module had a descent stage for landing on the Moon and an ascent stage to return astronauts to lunar orbit.\"},{\"id\":\"8.5\",\"text\":\"The design allowed launch by a single Saturn V rocket, and Apollo used technologies developed by Project Gemini and NASA semiconductor advances.\"}],\"entities\":[\"James Webb\",\"NASA\",\"Apollo spacecraft\",\"command module (CM)\",\"service module (SM)\",\"lunar module (LM)\",\"Saturn V\",\"Project Gemini\",\"Interplanetary Monitoring Platform (IMP)\",\"Apollo Guidance Computer (AGC)\"]},{\"chunkId\":9,\"points\":[{\"id\":\"9.1\",\"text\":\"The Apollo 1 fire on January 27, 1967 killed astronauts Gus Grissom, Ed White, and Roger B. Chaffee, and halted Project Apollo abruptly.\"},{\"id\":\"9.2\",\"text\":\"In October 1968, Apollo 7 evaluated the command module in Earth orbit.\"},{\"id\":\"9.3\",\"text\":\"In December 1968, Apollo 8 tested the command module in lunar orbit.\"},{\"id\":\"9.4\",\"text\":\"In March 1969, Apollo 9 put the lunar module through its paces in Earth orbit.\"},{\"id\":\"9.5\",\"text\":\"In May 1969, Apollo 10 conducted a 'dress rehearsal' in lunar orbit, leaving everything ready for Apollo 11's Moon landing by July 1969.\"}],\"entities\":[\"Project Apollo\",\"Apollo 1\",\"Gus Grissom\",\"Ed White\",\"Roger B. Chaffee\",\"Apollo 7\",\"Apollo 8\",\"Apollo 9\",\"Apollo 10\",\"Apollo 11\"]}],\"entities\":[\"Apollo 11\",\"NASA\",\"Apollo program\",\"Neil Armstrong\",\"Michael Collins\",\"Buzz Aldrin\",\"Saturn V\",\"Kennedy Space Center\",\"Lunar Module Eagle\",\"Command Module Columbia\",\"Sea of Tranquility\",\"Armstrong\",\"Aldrin\",\"Collins\",\"Richard Nixon\",\"Space Race\",\"United States\",\"Soviet Union\",\"John F. Kennedy\",\"Apollo 1\",\"Luna 15\",\"Columbia\",\"National Air and Space Museum\",\"Eagle\",\"Presidential Medal of Freedom\",\"Cold War\",\"Sputnik 1\",\"Sputnik crisis\",\"Dwight D. Eisenhower\",\"National Aeronautics and Space Administration (NASA)\",\"Project Mercury\",\"Yuri Gagarin\",\"Alan Shepard\",\"Kennedy\",\"Eisenhower\",\"United States Congress\",\"Moon\",\"Earth\",\"Rice University\",\"Houston, Texas\",\"Texas\",\"Norbert Wiener\",\"Massachusetts Institute of Technology\",\"Project Apollo\",\"Nikita Khrushchev\",\"United Nations General Assembly\",\"lunar orbit rendezvous\",\"James Webb\",\"Apollo spacecraft\",\"command module (CM)\",\"service module (SM)\",\"lunar module (LM)\",\"Project Gemini\",\"Interplanetary Monitoring Platform (IMP)\",\"Apollo Guidance Computer (AGC)\",\"Gus Grissom\",\"Ed White\",\"Roger B. Chaffee\",\"Apollo 7\",\"Apollo 8\",\"Apollo 9\",\"Apollo 10\"],\"findingCount\":9}}","seal":"d6e9a8ec87a0c30a27d4d9530199d0f30c5f0cd11eacc86364bc26056226d6be"}}

